course: politics
lesson: 34
title: Algorithms, bubbles and false balance
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: An app's feed is chosen by an algorithm, a set of rules a computer follows: it notices what you watch and for how long, then shows you more of the same.
explain: It is like a dinner lady who sees you take chips once and from then on piles only chips on your plate: she is not reading your mind, she is counting what you took, and soon you forget other food exists.
explain: When your feed and friends all agree with you, it can feel as if everyone does; that is a bubble, or echo chamber, and posts that make people angry spread fastest because angry people comment.
explain: TV can mislead in the opposite way: putting one scientist and one doubter side by side can make a question look 50-50 when the experts mostly agree; that is false balance.
explain: You can step out of the bubble: follow some people you disagree with, read news from other places, and notice when your feed keeps getting stranger.
fact: An algorithm is a set of rules a computer follows; feeds use it to choose what to show next from what you watched and clicked.
fact: When you only hear opinions like your own, you are in an echo chamber or filter bubble, and it can feel as if everyone agrees with you.
fact: Researchers disagree about how strong bubbles are, and many people still meet other views at school, at work and in their families.
fact: Posts that make people angry get many comments and shares, so apps show them to more people; posts made to anger you on purpose are called rage bait.
fact: False balance means showing two sides as equal when the experts are not split; most climate scientists agree the Earth is warming because of people.
fact: Following someone you disagree with lets you hear what they really say, not what others say they say.
fact: A rabbit hole is going deeper into one strange topic, and researchers argue about how often feeds lead people there.
fact: Each person's feed is made for them, so two people can see very different things about the same news.
fact: Liking or sharing tells the algorithm to show more and tells friends you agree; you can like a creator without sharing everything they post.
fact: "Nobody is talking about this!" is a hook to make you feel you found a secret, even when millions have already watched.
fact: Ways to break a bubble include reading different outlets, news from another country, and the other side's best argument.
fact: Words in this lesson: "it's giving" means "it looks like" or "it feels like"; "highkey" means openly or very; "fixin' to" in the US South means about to; "gurt" in the West Country means big.
exercise: Kai watched one car video and now his feed is all cars. Why? | An algorithm noticed what he watched and showed more of it | The app read his mind | His friends chose his videos for him without telling him, as a joke on him | Car companies paid him to watch them
exercise: What is an algorithm? | A set of rules a computer follows to make a choice | A secret person who picks every video | A kind of computer virus | The name of a famous video creator
exercise: Priya's dad only watches videos that agree with him and says "Everyone thinks like me!" What is he in? | An echo chamber, or bubble | A fact-check | A public debate | A secret ballot
exercise: Why do angry posts often get the most comments and get shown more? | Apps count comments and shares and show busy posts to more people | Angry posts are always the most true | Apps are told by law to show angry posts at the top of every single feed, every day | Only angry people use social media
exercise: What is rage bait? | Posts made to make you angry so you react | Food used for catching fish | A calm report of the facts | A correction made by a newspaper
exercise: A TV debate gives equal time to one scientist who says the Earth is warming and one person who says no. What is the risk? | False balance, as if experts were split 50-50 | Too much detail about the weather | No risk, it shows exactly how experts are split | The scientist gets more time than is fair
exercise: What do most climate scientists agree on? | The Earth is warming because of people | The Earth is getting a bit colder every year | Nobody knows if the Earth's temperature changes | The Earth's temperature never changes at all
exercise: Why does Aroha's mum follow a politician she disagrees with? | To know what they actually say, not what others say they say | Because she secretly agrees with everything they say | Because the app forces her to follow them | To post angry comments under every post they make, so that the politician sees them
exercise: Dylan watched a few conspiracy videos for fun and now his feed is full of stranger ones. What is that called? | Going down a rabbit hole | Breaking right out of a bubble | A fact-check of the videos | A correction made to his feed
exercise: Fatima and her friend see totally different posts about the same story. Why? | Each feed is personalised for each person | One of their phones is broken and showing old posts | One of them is looking at a fake copy of the app | The news story changed between their two clicks
exercise: Oisin shares every post from his favourite gaming creator, even one about politics he doesn't understand. What is a good habit? | You can like a creator and still not share everything | Always share everything from creators you like, because they have checked it all first | Never watch any creator ever again | Only share the posts you do not understand
exercise: A video saying "Nobody is talking about this!" has ten million views. What is going on? | It is a hook to make you feel you found a secret | Nobody has really watched it, the numbers are made up | The video is hidden from everyone else except for you | It must be true, because it is a secret that is hidden
exercise: How can Tyler's class break a bubble? | Read news from a different country each week | Only follow people who agree with them | Turn off all news forever | Watch only one channel every day
exercise: What happens when you like or share a post? | The algorithm shows you more like it | The post is checked for truth by the app | The post is deleted from the app for good | Your feed shows you fewer posts like it
test: "it's giving everyone agrees with me, my whole feed says so" — what is the best reply? | Your feed is picked for you, so it may be a bubble | Then everyone in the world must agree | Feeds always show every side equally | Feeds show only what is true
test: In Georgia: "Y'all, I'm fixin' to share this, nobody's talkin' about it!" It has 10 million views. What is wrong? | Ten million views means lots of people already are | Nothing, secret videos are always true | It should be shared to 10 million more first, so that the secret gets out before it is deleted | Views on videos are never counted
test: What would happen if Kai liked every car video he saw for a month? | His feed would fill up even more with car videos | His feed would show him only cooking videos | The app would stop showing him any videos | His feed would show every topic equally
test: A TV show puts one climate scientist against one doubter and says "the jury's out". What is the better version? | Most climate scientists agree, so equal time can mislead | It is a fair 50-50 split among scientists, so giving each side the same time is fair | Nobody has studied the climate at all | Doubters are the larger group of experts
test: "highkey the angriest posts are the truest, that's why they're at the top" — what is the best answer? | They are at the top because anger brings comments, not because they are true | Yes, apps check truth before showing posts, so the ones at the top have passed the most checks | Yes, angry people never post false things | Yes, the top post is always the truest one
test: In Bristol: "Gurt big rabbit hole, my feed's all aliens-built-the-pyramids now, proper job." What should he notice? | His feed is getting stranger, so step back and look elsewhere | It proves aliens built the pyramids, because so many different videos say the same thing | Feeds only show what experts agree on | Rabbit holes are always about real rabbits
test: A boy says, "I don't follow anyone I disagree with, that's how you stay smart." What is a better idea? | Hearing the other side from the other side helps you understand it | He is right, the other side has nothing to teach, so following them is just a waste of time | Only angry people follow the other side | Following others means you must agree with them
test: In a cartoon, a magic mirror only ever tells the queen she is right and everyone loves her. What is that like? | An echo chamber that only repeats your own view | A fact-checker giving a fair rating | A public debate with two sides | A correction of the queen's mistakes
test: Fatima says, "It's all over my feed, so everyone's seen it." What is true? | Feeds are personalised, so others may not see it at all | Every feed shows the same posts to everyone, in the same order, at the same time | Only famous people see different feeds | Feeds are chosen by the government
test: Which is a fair thing to say about bubbles? | Researchers disagree on how strong they are | Every scientist says bubbles control everyone | Bubbles only happen to grown-ups, never to kids | Bubbles cannot happen online, only in real life
test: "shut up and share it, everyone in our group already did" — the post is about politics you don't understand. What is fair? | You can choose not to share what you do not understand | You must share anything your group shares, or you will be thrown out of the group by the app | Sharing proves you understand it | Not sharing is against the app's rules
exam: Which of the following most accurately characterises the function of an algorithm in determining the content of a feed? | The selection of content in accordance with rules applied to the user's previous viewing | The reading of the user's thoughts by artificial means | The selection of content by the user's acquaintances, who are notified of each item and approve it in advance | The random selection of content without regard to the user
exam: Identify the term for the presentation of two positions as equally supported where the evidence is predominantly on one side. | False balance | Satire | A correction | Astroturfing
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the scientific consensus on the warming of the Earth. | The majority of climate scientists hold that the Earth is warming owing to human activity | The majority of climate scientists hold that the Earth is cooling | Climate scientists are evenly divided upon the question, and the evidence favours neither position | No investigation of the question has been undertaken
exam: Whereas a person wishes to diminish the effect of an echo chamber, determine the course of action most appropriate. | The consultation of diverse sources, including the strongest arguments of opposing views | The exclusive consultation of sources with which the person agrees, so as to avoid any confusion | The sharing of all content received from familiar accounts | The abandonment of all sources of news whatsoever
